WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.300
Welcome to Session ten.

2
00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:11.550
The subject today is convolutional neural networks: where they came from, what assumption they build in, and what that assumption buys you in biology.

3
00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:19.525
This is the lecture, recorded, so you can listen to it before the session, or after it, or instead of reading, or while you are on the bus.

4
00:00:19.525 --> 00:00:22.275
It follows the slides exactly.

5
00:00:22.275 --> 00:00:30.550
Everything in it is written out in full on the Session ten page of the course book, so if a number goes past too quickly, the page has it.

6
00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:33.200
Let me say at the start where we are.

7
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.900
In Sessions eight and nine you built neural networks.

8
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:47.825
A network takes numbers in, multiplies them by weights, adds them up, passes the result through a simple non-linear function, and repeats that a few times until it produces an answer.

9
00:00:47.825 --> 00:01:00.975
The weights are the things the network learns, and Session nine covered how it learns them: backpropagation works out how much each individual weight contributed to the error, and then nudges it in the direction that reduces the error.

10
00:01:00.975 --> 00:01:04.950
Those networks were what we call dense, or fully connected.

11
00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:11.450
Every input is wired to every unit in the next layer, and every one of those wires has its own weight.

12
00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:14.600
That is the most general thing you can build.

13
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:23.025
Today is about why the most general thing is usually the wrong thing, and about what you get when you give the network an assumption instead of freedom.

14
00:01:23.025 --> 00:01:25.525
We will do this in three blocks.

15
00:01:25.525 --> 00:01:30.175
First, why convolution works, and the sixty-year story of how it got there.

16
00:01:30.175 --> 00:01:35.950
Second, convolutional networks on biological sequences, DNA and protein.

17
00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:42.300
Third, convolutional networks on images, from a cancer slide to a model of the whole human cell.

18
00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:47.475
Here is the entire session in one picture and in one sentence.

19
00:01:47.475 --> 00:01:51.825
If you remember nothing else from the next ninety minutes, remember this.

20
00:01:51.825 --> 00:01:56.375
A dense layer learns a separate weight for every position of its input.

21
00:01:56.375 --> 00:02:04.400
A convolutional layer assumes that nearby inputs belong together, and reuses the same small detector at every position.

22
00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:06.850
Look at the picture on the slide.

23
00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:14.075
On the left, the dense layer: every input position has its own line to the unit, and every line has its own weight.

24
00:02:14.075 --> 00:02:25.325
If you want the network to notice a particular pattern, it has to learn that pattern once for position one, again for position two, again for position three, and so on, all the way along.

25
00:02:25.325 --> 00:02:28.925
Nothing it learns in one place helps it anywhere else.

26
00:02:28.925 --> 00:02:34.450
On the right, the convolution: one small set of weights, drawn once, and then slid along.

27
00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:37.650
The same weights are applied at every position.

28
00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:41.925
The network learns the pattern once and can then find it anywhere.

29
00:02:41.925 --> 00:02:44.250
That is the whole idea.

30
00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:47.175
And notice what kind of idea it is.

31
00:02:47.175 --> 00:02:52.100
It is not a cleverer learning algorithm, and it is not more computing power.

32
00:02:52.100 --> 00:03:00.875
It is an assumption about the data, built into the shape of the model: that what matters is local, and that it means the same thing wherever it occurs.

33
00:03:00.875 --> 00:03:09.800
When that assumption is true of your data, you get a model that needs far fewer weights, and therefore far less data, than anything we have built so far.

34
00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:13.050
Keep coming back to this sentence as we go.

35
00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:22.725
Every result in this session is either that assumption paying off, or that assumption failing at the one place where it does not hold, which is position in proteins.

36
00:03:22.725 --> 00:03:24.600
We will meet both.

37
00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.675
Block one.

38
00:03:26.675 --> 00:03:31.100
Where convolutional networks came from, and why convolution works.

39
00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:34.850
On the page, this block is the section "Why convolution?".

40
00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:47.650
In this block we do the history first, because the history explains something important about the whole field, and then we do the mechanics: what a convolutional layer actually computes, and the four settings that control it.

41
00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:54.425
The idea behind convolutional networks is more than sixty years old, and it starts with cats.

42
00:03:54.425 --> 00:04:09.150
In the late nineteen fifties, David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel put fine electrodes into the visual cortex of cats, and recorded what single neurons did while the cat was shown simple patterns: spots of light, bars, edges at different angles.

43
00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:12.025
They found two things that matter for us.

44
00:04:12.025 --> 00:04:18.825
The first is that each neuron responded only to a small part of the visual field, not to the whole picture.

45
00:04:18.825 --> 00:04:24.450
That small part is the neuron's receptive field, and that is where the term comes from.

46
00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:27.350
We will use it again later in this block.

47
00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:38.425
The second is that within its own small patch, a neuron responded only to one particular feature, for example an edge at one particular angle, and not to an edge at a different angle.

48
00:04:38.425 --> 00:04:41.325
And there were two kinds of such neurons.

49
00:04:41.325 --> 00:04:46.300
What they called simple cells responded to an edge at one exact position.

50
00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:52.625
What they called complex cells responded to that same edge anywhere within a somewhat larger region.

51
00:04:52.625 --> 00:04:59.925
Stop and look at that for a moment, because those two cell types are exactly the two operations of a convolutional network.

52
00:04:59.925 --> 00:05:05.025
A simple cell is a local feature detector, which is a convolution filter.

53
00:05:05.025 --> 00:05:11.250
A complex cell is that detector followed by a step that tolerates small movements, which is pooling.

54
00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:15.925
A convolutional layer was described in a cat in nineteen sixty-two.

55
00:05:15.925 --> 00:05:20.650
Hubel and Wiesel received the Nobel Prize for it in nineteen eighty-one.

56
00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:29.475
In nineteen eighty, Kunihiko Fukushima built both operations into a layered artificial network, which he called the Neocognitron.

57
00:05:29.475 --> 00:05:38.700
The title of that paper actually promises pattern recognition unaffected by shift in position, which is the property we will shortly call invariance.

58
00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:42.250
So by nineteen eighty the architecture existed.

59
00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:45.500
What it lacked was a good way to learn its weights.

60
00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:56.825
That arrived at Bell Labs, where Yann LeCun and colleagues trained a convolutional network with backpropagation, Session nine's algorithm, to read the handwritten postcodes on American mail.

61
00:05:56.825 --> 00:05:59.375
That was nineteen eighty-nine.

62
00:05:59.375 --> 00:06:11.625
Its successor, LeNet-5, published in nineteen ninety-eight, read the amounts written on bank cheques, and by the late nineteen nineties it was reading a large share of all the cheques written in the United States.

63
00:06:11.625 --> 00:06:17.975
So: by nineteen ninety-eight, convolutional networks were in production, in banks, reading real money.

64
00:06:17.975 --> 00:06:20.850
And then, for about a decade, nothing.

65
00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:26.300
Through the two thousands, computer vision belonged to what are called hand-crafted features.

66
00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:36.475
That means a person sat down and designed, by hand, a recipe for turning an image into a few hundred numbers: how many edges at this angle here, how much texture there.

67
00:06:36.475 --> 00:06:48.125
Those numbers were then fed to a classifier, usually a support vector machine, which is a relative of Session seven's perceptron that separates the classes with the boundary leaving the widest margin.

68
00:06:48.125 --> 00:06:53.125
Neural networks were widely thought to be hard to train and not worth the trouble.

69
00:06:53.125 --> 00:06:57.825
And here is the thing: given what people had, that judgement was correct.

70
00:06:57.825 --> 00:07:01.550
The datasets of the time held a few thousand images.

71
00:07:01.550 --> 00:07:09.375
On a few thousand images, a well-designed hand-made feature really does beat a network that has to learn everything from scratch.

72
00:07:09.375 --> 00:07:11.850
The problem was not the idea.

73
00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:16.050
Two ingredients were missing, and neither of them was an idea.

74
00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:18.275
Data, and compute.

75
00:07:18.875 --> 00:07:29.625
The data arrived in two thousand and nine, from Fei-Fei Li's group, and it was called ImageNet: millions of photographs, labelled by thousands of crowd-workers paid per image.

76
00:07:29.625 --> 00:07:33.625
From twenty ten there was an annual challenge built on it.

77
00:07:33.625 --> 00:07:38.450
Sort one point two million training photographs into a thousand classes.

78
00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:46.075
The score was the top-five error: the fraction of test photographs whose true class is not among the program's five best guesses.

79
00:07:46.075 --> 00:07:48.000
Lower is better.

80
00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:56.475
The first two winners, in twenty ten and twenty eleven, used hand-crafted features, and reached twenty-eight per cent and twenty-six per cent.

81
00:07:56.475 --> 00:07:58.525
Then twenty twelve.

82
00:07:58.525 --> 00:08:08.625
Alex Krizhevsky, a PhD student of Geoffrey Hinton in Toronto, together with Ilya Sutskever, entered a convolutional network with sixty million weights.

83
00:08:08.625 --> 00:08:13.025
He trained it for five to six days on two gaming graphics cards.

84
00:08:13.025 --> 00:08:21.475
Its top-five error was sixteen point four per cent on the challenge's own training data, against twenty-six point two per cent for the runner-up.

85
00:08:21.475 --> 00:08:23.900
That is not an improvement.

86
00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:26.200
That is a different sport.

87
00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:31.875
Ten percentage points is the largest jump the challenge ever saw, before or after.

88
00:08:31.875 --> 00:08:37.750
Now look at the bullets on the slide, because the point I want you to take from AlexNet is not the number.

89
00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:40.200
It is which parts of it were new.

90
00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:44.350
Convolution came from LeNet, more than twenty years earlier.

91
00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:52.650
The ReLU, which is simply the rule that a negative number becomes zero, instead of the older smooth S-shaped function: Session nine.

92
00:08:52.650 --> 00:09:00.600
Dropout, which switches off random units during training so that the network cannot lean on any single one of them: Session nine.

93
00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:05.500
Data augmentation, which we come back to in Block three: not new either.

94
00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:09.000
Almost nothing in AlexNet was new on its own.

95
00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:11.400
What was new was the scale.

96
00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:15.275
ImageNet's data, and graphics cards to compute on.

97
00:09:15.275 --> 00:09:26.650
That is the lesson of this whole story, and it is worth carrying out of this course: a good idea can be right and still fail for thirty years, because the conditions it needs do not exist yet.

98
00:09:26.650 --> 00:09:28.375
Two footnotes.

99
00:09:28.375 --> 00:09:33.775
Within two years, almost every entry in the challenge was a convolutional network.

100
00:09:33.775 --> 00:09:41.500
And Geoffrey Hinton shared the twenty twenty-four Nobel Prize in Physics with John Hopfield, for the foundations of all of this.

101
00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:44.825
After AlexNet, the winners mainly went deeper.

102
00:09:44.825 --> 00:09:50.175
VGG used stacks of up to nineteen layers of small three-by-three convolutions.

103
00:09:50.175 --> 00:09:53.175
GoogLeNet won with twenty-two layers.

104
00:09:53.175 --> 00:09:58.375
And then simply adding layers stopped working, for a reason that surprised everyone.

105
00:09:58.375 --> 00:10:02.300
We come back to that in Block three, when we meet ResNet.

106
00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:08.125
One more thing, and I want to be careful about it, because the easy version of this story is wrong.

107
00:10:08.125 --> 00:10:11.775
People will tell you that convolutional networks won.

108
00:10:11.775 --> 00:10:14.775
They did not, or at least not permanently.

109
00:10:14.775 --> 00:10:31.725
In images they were ahead for about five years, and then the Vision Transformer arrived: cut the picture into patches, treat the patches as a sequence, build in none of convolution's assumptions at all, and, given enough data, match and pass the convolutional networks on the very tasks they had owned.

110
00:10:31.725 --> 00:10:38.350
The strongest model in your own lab today, DINOv2, is one of those, not a convolutional network.

111
00:10:38.350 --> 00:10:41.575
And in biology nothing took over at all.

112
00:10:41.575 --> 00:10:50.900
Convolution sits next to the sequence models of Session eleven and the structure models of Session twelve, each with its own assumption about what matters.

113
00:10:50.900 --> 00:10:55.575
So the lesson of ImageNet is not that convolution is the right answer.

114
00:10:55.575 --> 00:11:03.400
It is that building in an assumption is worth a great deal when you do not have much data, and worth less and less as the data grows.

115
00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:09.125
In biology we usually do not have much data, which is why this session is still useful.

116
00:11:09.125 --> 00:11:12.600
Keep that trade-off in your head for the rest of the course.

117
00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:18.100
Let me spend a slide on the hardware, because without it none of the rest happened.

118
00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:20.975
What does a network actually compute?

119
00:11:20.975 --> 00:11:29.650
A convolution, or a dense layer, is a very large number of multiply-and-add operations, and, crucially, they do not depend on each other.

120
00:11:29.650 --> 00:11:36.175
Multiplying weight one by input one does not need the result of multiplying weight two by input two.

121
00:11:36.175 --> 00:11:38.800
They can all be done at the same time.

122
00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:47.275
A normal computer processor is built the other way round: a few very fast cores, each doing one complicated thing after another.

123
00:11:47.275 --> 00:11:56.500
A graphics card is built for drawing millions of pixels at once, so it has thousands of simple cores, each doing one easy thing, all at the same time.

124
00:11:56.500 --> 00:12:00.725
Which is precisely the shape of the arithmetic a network needs.

125
00:12:00.725 --> 00:12:05.000
That coincidence is most of modern artificial intelligence.

126
00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:15.300
It became usable for general computing when NVIDIA released CUDA in two thousand and seven, which let people program graphics cards for something other than graphics.

127
00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:28.700
In two thousand and nine, Raina, Madhavan and Ng trained a four-layer network with a hundred million parameters and reported that the graphics card took the training time, in their words, from several weeks to around a single day.

128
00:12:28.700 --> 00:12:37.300
AlexNet, three years later, was trained on two GTX five-eighty cards with three gigabytes of memory each, for five to six days.

129
00:12:37.300 --> 00:12:44.900
Its architecture was actually limited by that memory: the network was split across the two cards because it did not fit on one.

130
00:12:44.900 --> 00:12:49.200
And the same idea scales straight up to today's data centres.

131
00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:55.500
The classroom version of it is what you use in the lab: the course server, and Colab's free graphics card.

132
00:12:56.100 --> 00:12:58.075
Now the mechanics.

133
00:12:58.075 --> 00:13:03.750
Let us count, because the argument for convolution is at bottom an argument about counting.

134
00:13:03.750 --> 00:13:08.775
Every model in this course so far gives every input position its own weight.

135
00:13:08.775 --> 00:13:12.475
Take the protein sequences of Sessions eight and nine.

136
00:13:12.475 --> 00:13:26.700
A hundred and fifty residues, and twenty possible amino acids at each one, which is how you write a sequence as numbers: twenty numbers per position, nineteen of them zero and a one in the slot for the amino acid that is actually there.

137
00:13:26.700 --> 00:13:29.750
That is three thousand numbers going in.

138
00:13:29.750 --> 00:13:34.375
So a dense layer needs three thousand weights for every single hidden unit.

139
00:13:34.375 --> 00:13:42.750
For a colour image of two hundred and twenty-four by two hundred and twenty-four pixels, it is over a hundred and fifty thousand weights per unit.

140
00:13:42.750 --> 00:13:46.100
But the waste is not really the size of the number.

141
00:13:46.100 --> 00:13:49.175
The waste is what those weights are spent on.

142
00:13:49.175 --> 00:13:55.750
If a hydrophobic stretch matters at position ten, the network learns a detector for it at position ten.

143
00:13:55.750 --> 00:13:59.750
Then it learns that same detector again at position eleven.

144
00:13:59.750 --> 00:14:01.875
And again at twelve.

145
00:14:01.875 --> 00:14:12.825
Neighbouring residues are treated as being exactly as unrelated as distant ones, because the dense layer does not know that position ten and position eleven are next to each other.

146
00:14:12.825 --> 00:14:16.225
It has no idea the input has an order at all.

147
00:14:16.225 --> 00:14:18.325
Now the convolution.

148
00:14:18.325 --> 00:14:22.675
The slide has the comparison on our own localisation data.

149
00:14:22.675 --> 00:14:33.725
A dense layer, three thousand inputs to sixty-four units, is three thousand times sixty-four, plus sixty-four biases: a hundred and ninety-two thousand and sixty-four weights.

150
00:14:33.725 --> 00:14:46.525
A convolutional layer, taking twenty channels to thirty-two filters with a window seven residues wide, is seven times twenty times thirty-two, plus thirty-two biases: four thousand five hundred and twelve.

151
00:14:46.525 --> 00:14:48.625
Forty times fewer.

152
00:14:48.625 --> 00:14:55.100
And here is the part I would underline twice: it stays four and a half thousand for a protein of any length.

153
00:14:55.100 --> 00:14:58.325
The dense layer's count grows with the input.

154
00:14:58.325 --> 00:15:03.525
The convolution's does not, because the weights are reused rather than repeated.

155
00:15:03.525 --> 00:15:07.450
One honest complication, which you will meet in the lab.

156
00:15:07.450 --> 00:15:10.925
Whole networks are less simple than single layers.

157
00:15:10.925 --> 00:15:17.100
In the lab you will find that adding a convolutional layer sometimes makes the whole network smaller.

158
00:15:17.100 --> 00:15:27.200
That sounds backwards until you see why: the convolution, with its pooling, hands a smaller feature map to the dense layer that follows, and the dense layer is where the weights were.

159
00:15:27.800 --> 00:15:37.825
Before we go further I want to be concrete about shapes, because half the confusion with convolutional networks is not understanding what the numbers flowing through them look like.

160
00:15:37.825 --> 00:15:43.750
A batch of images is a block of numbers with four dimensions: B by C by H by W.

161
00:15:43.750 --> 00:15:47.825
B is the batch, how many images you are processing at once.

162
00:15:47.825 --> 00:15:52.575
C is the channels, which for a colour image is three, for red, green and blue.

163
00:15:52.575 --> 00:15:56.000
H and W are height and width in pixels.

164
00:15:56.000 --> 00:15:59.725
A batch of sequences has three: B by C by L.

165
00:15:59.725 --> 00:16:01.900
B is again the batch.

166
00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:04.500
L is the length of the sequence.

167
00:16:04.500 --> 00:16:12.750
And C, the channels, is twenty for a one-hot protein sequence, one channel per amino acid, or four for DNA, one per base.

168
00:16:12.750 --> 00:16:17.725
So a channel is one of the parallel streams of numbers available at each position.

169
00:16:17.725 --> 00:16:26.775
At the input they have a concrete meaning: this channel is how much leucine there is here, which is one if the residue is a leucine and zero otherwise.

170
00:16:26.775 --> 00:16:32.600
Deeper in the network they stop being nameable, and become whatever the network found useful.

171
00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:36.125
Now the kernel, which is the small detector itself.

172
00:16:36.125 --> 00:16:44.000
A size-seven kernel on protein has twenty times seven weights: one weight per amino acid per position in its window.

173
00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:55.375
It sits over residues one to seven, multiplies and adds, producing one number; then it steps along and sits over residues two to eight, producing the next number; and so on to the end.

174
00:16:55.375 --> 00:17:01.525
What comes out is a row of numbers, one per position, saying how strongly this detector fired there.

175
00:17:01.525 --> 00:17:05.625
That row has a name we will use a lot: it is a feature map.

176
00:17:05.625 --> 00:17:09.150
And a layer does not have one kernel, it has many.

177
00:17:09.150 --> 00:17:15.825
Each kernel is called a filter, and each filter produces one output channel: one learned feature type.

178
00:17:15.825 --> 00:17:24.425
Thirty-two filters means thirty-two different detectors, each free to learn to look for something different, and thirty-two feature maps coming out.

179
00:17:25.025 --> 00:17:31.550
Four settings control a convolutional layer, and you need all four to read any architecture in a paper.

180
00:17:31.550 --> 00:17:37.675
Kernel size is the width of the local window: three pixels by three, or seven residues.

181
00:17:37.675 --> 00:17:41.925
Bigger means each unit sees more at once, and costs more weights.

182
00:17:41.925 --> 00:17:48.075
Padding is adding zeros at the edges of the input, so that the output can keep the input's length.

183
00:17:48.075 --> 00:17:53.350
Without it the output is shorter than the input, which we will look at on the next slide.

184
00:17:53.350 --> 00:17:56.925
Stride is how far the kernel moves between steps.

185
00:17:56.925 --> 00:18:00.475
Stride one means it is applied at every position.

186
00:18:00.475 --> 00:18:08.075
Stride two means it skips every other position, so the output is half as long, which is a cheap way of shrinking the data.

187
00:18:08.075 --> 00:18:13.875
Pooling replaces small regions by a single number, usually their maximum or their mean.

188
00:18:13.875 --> 00:18:19.300
Two-by-two max pooling takes each little square of four and keeps only the largest.

189
00:18:19.300 --> 00:18:26.375
It shrinks the feature map, and, as we will see, it also buys a small amount of tolerance to the input moving slightly.

190
00:18:26.375 --> 00:18:31.325
Those four, plus the number of filters, are essentially the whole vocabulary.

191
00:18:31.925 --> 00:18:34.550
Here is why padding exists.

192
00:18:34.550 --> 00:18:37.750
Take a kernel of size five, with no padding.

193
00:18:37.750 --> 00:18:48.975
The kernel needs five positions under it to produce one output, so it cannot be centred on the first two pixels or the last two: you lose four pixels of width and four of height, every layer.

194
00:18:48.975 --> 00:18:51.925
On a small image that matters quickly.

195
00:18:51.925 --> 00:18:59.650
A twenty-eight by twenty-eight image, which is what the blood cells in the lab are, loses four each time: twenty-four, twenty, sixteen.

196
00:18:59.650 --> 00:19:07.100
You run out of image after a handful of layers, and one of the lab's questions asks you to work out exactly how many fit.

197
00:19:07.100 --> 00:19:12.750
With padding, you add a border of zeros so the output has the same size as the input.

198
00:19:12.750 --> 00:19:21.075
And once the size stops shrinking, depth becomes free: you can stack as many layers as you like without the data disappearing underneath you.

199
00:19:21.075 --> 00:19:25.250
That is what makes a network of a hundred layers possible at all.

200
00:19:25.850 --> 00:19:32.550
Now we come to the most important idea in this block, and the one that causes the most confusion every year.

201
00:19:32.550 --> 00:19:38.575
It is a pair of words that sound similar and mean opposite things: equivariance and invariance.

202
00:19:38.575 --> 00:19:41.600
Let me set it up with a concrete example.

203
00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:52.700
Imagine a detector sliding along a piece of DNA, looking for a TATA box, which is a short specific run of bases that marks where the machinery that reads a gene should start.

204
00:19:52.700 --> 00:19:56.300
The detector produces a score at every position.

205
00:19:56.300 --> 00:20:01.850
Now take the same sequence and move the TATA box eight positions further along.

206
00:20:01.850 --> 00:20:04.200
What happens to the scores?

207
00:20:04.200 --> 00:20:06.800
The peak moves eight positions.

208
00:20:06.800 --> 00:20:11.500
The whole pattern of output shifts by exactly the amount the input shifted.

209
00:20:11.500 --> 00:20:13.725
That is equivariance.

210
00:20:13.725 --> 00:20:17.500
The output changes in the same way the input changed.

211
00:20:17.500 --> 00:20:27.075
In symbols, if T is the shift and f is the model, equivariance means f of T x equals T of f of x: shifting the input shifts the output.

212
00:20:27.075 --> 00:20:30.500
An equivariant output answers the question where.

213
00:20:30.500 --> 00:20:33.025
Invariance is the other one.

214
00:20:33.025 --> 00:20:36.625
An output is invariant if it does not change at all.

215
00:20:36.625 --> 00:20:44.175
The single answer to does this sequence contain a TATA box is yes, and it is still yes after you move the motif.

216
00:20:44.175 --> 00:20:47.125
f of T x equals f of x.

217
00:20:47.125 --> 00:20:51.875
An invariant output answers the question whether, or what, but not where.

218
00:20:51.875 --> 00:20:58.875
Which of the two you want depends entirely on the question you are asking, and this is worth remembering for the exam.

219
00:20:58.875 --> 00:21:10.075
If you are labelling every residue, is this residue part of a signal peptide, what is the secondary structure here, you want equivariance: shift the sequence, and the labels should shift with it.

220
00:21:10.075 --> 00:21:22.075
If you are labelling the whole input, which compartment of the cell is this protein in, what type of blood cell is this, you want invariance: a lymphocyte is a lymphocyte wherever it sits in the picture.

221
00:21:22.075 --> 00:21:24.850
So which does convolution give you?

222
00:21:24.850 --> 00:21:27.975
Equivariance, and it gives it to you for free.

223
00:21:27.975 --> 00:21:34.950
If the input shifts, the shared kernel finds the same pattern somewhere else, so its output shifts by the same amount.

224
00:21:34.950 --> 00:21:42.100
And notice where that comes from: it comes from weight sharing itself, from the fact that the same weights are used everywhere.

225
00:21:42.100 --> 00:21:45.075
It is not something the network learns.

226
00:21:45.075 --> 00:21:54.150
The figure on the slide shows a random, untrained one-dimensional convolution applied to a signal, and to the same signal shifted by ten positions.

227
00:21:54.150 --> 00:22:02.375
The shifted input's output is the original output shifted by ten, and away from the edges the difference is zero point zero zero.

228
00:22:02.375 --> 00:22:05.125
The network has not been trained at all.

229
00:22:05.125 --> 00:22:08.025
Equivariance was there from the start.

230
00:22:08.025 --> 00:22:12.925
And now the sentence to write down: equivariance is not invariance.

231
00:22:12.925 --> 00:22:15.875
The feature is found wherever it is.

232
00:22:15.875 --> 00:22:22.375
But where it was found still differs, and what the rest of the network does with that difference is a separate question.

233
00:22:22.375 --> 00:22:24.600
That is the next slide.

234
00:22:25.200 --> 00:22:30.775
So let us trace where position information actually goes in a network, step by step.

235
00:22:30.775 --> 00:22:35.325
There are four steps on this slide, and each does something different with it.

236
00:22:35.325 --> 00:22:38.250
Step one, the convolution itself.

237
00:22:38.250 --> 00:22:41.050
The feature map shifts with the input.

238
00:22:41.050 --> 00:22:44.200
Position is fully preserved, just moved.

239
00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:45.975
Equivariant.

240
00:22:45.975 --> 00:22:50.300
Step two, local pooling, for example two-by-two max pooling.

241
00:22:50.300 --> 00:22:58.300
If a pattern moves by one pixel, it quite often stays inside the same pooling window, and then the pooled output does not change.

242
00:22:58.300 --> 00:23:02.275
So local pooling buys you some tolerance to small shifts.

243
00:23:02.275 --> 00:23:05.100
Notice the words: some tolerance.

244
00:23:05.100 --> 00:23:07.150
Not invariance.

245
00:23:07.150 --> 00:23:11.875
Move far enough and you cross into the next window and the output changes.

246
00:23:11.875 --> 00:23:15.225
Step three, flatten followed by a dense layer.

247
00:23:15.225 --> 00:23:23.000
Flatten means taking the whole feature map and laying it out as one long list of numbers, which is then fed to a dense layer.

248
00:23:23.000 --> 00:23:28.825
And look what that does: every position of the final feature map gets its own weight again.

249
00:23:28.825 --> 00:23:32.400
You have just thrown away the thing convolution bought you.

250
00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:41.525
The network can now say this feature was present at position seventeen, specifically, which is sometimes exactly what you want, and sometimes a disaster.

251
00:23:41.525 --> 00:23:44.400
Step four, global average pooling.

252
00:23:44.400 --> 00:23:50.600
Average each feature map over every position, so that each filter is reduced to a single number.

253
00:23:50.600 --> 00:23:55.950
The network can then say this feature was present somewhere in the input, and nothing more.

254
00:23:55.950 --> 00:24:02.275
That is a position-invariant summary, and it is what our localisation network in Block two uses.

255
00:24:02.275 --> 00:24:10.400
So the convolution is equivariant, and which of these later steps you choose is what decides whether the whole network cares about position.

256
00:24:10.400 --> 00:24:13.600
That is a design decision, and you make it.

257
00:24:13.600 --> 00:24:16.475
One warning before we leave this slide.

258
00:24:16.475 --> 00:24:19.425
Everything I have said is about shifts.

259
00:24:19.425 --> 00:24:27.900
Rotation is a completely different matter: nothing in convolution, and nothing in pooling, gives you any tolerance to rotation at all.

260
00:24:27.900 --> 00:24:31.375
We will measure exactly how badly in Block three.

261
00:24:31.975 --> 00:24:38.300
This is what step three looks like when it goes wrong, and I like this experiment because it is so blunt.

262
00:24:38.300 --> 00:24:45.275
In the lab, a convolutional network is trained on blood cells that have all been pasted into one corner of the image.

263
00:24:45.275 --> 00:24:48.975
Every single training image has the cell in the corner.

264
00:24:48.975 --> 00:24:58.600
The network trains happily and reaches between nought point seven seven and nought point eight six accuracy on test images that also have the cell in the corner.

265
00:24:58.600 --> 00:25:02.575
Then you show it the same cells placed at random positions.

266
00:25:02.575 --> 00:25:07.175
Accuracy falls to between nought point one eight and nought point two nine.

267
00:25:07.175 --> 00:25:13.250
To put that in context, always guessing the most common class scores nought point one nine eight.

268
00:25:13.250 --> 00:25:15.750
The network has gone to chance.

269
00:25:15.750 --> 00:25:17.225
Now, why?

270
00:25:17.225 --> 00:25:21.575
The convolution is equivariant, so the features are still found.

271
00:25:21.575 --> 00:25:25.525
The cell is detected perfectly well in its new position.

272
00:25:25.525 --> 00:25:36.350
The failure is entirely in the last step: flatten plus a dense layer gives every position its own weight, and the weights belonging to the middle of the image were never trained on anything.

273
00:25:36.350 --> 00:25:40.325
The features arrive, and meet weights that have never seen a cell.

274
00:25:40.325 --> 00:25:43.625
There are two fixes, and they are different in kind.

275
00:25:43.625 --> 00:25:50.050
One: replace flatten with global average pooling, so that position stops mattering by construction.

276
00:25:50.050 --> 00:25:55.425
Two: train on cells at all positions, so that every position's weights get trained.

277
00:25:55.425 --> 00:26:01.375
With randomly placed training cells, the three-block network reaches nought point seven two two.

278
00:26:01.375 --> 00:26:09.575
This is the clearest demonstration I know that equivariance is not invariance, and it is why that distinction is not word-play.

279
00:26:10.175 --> 00:26:15.400
Last idea of Block one, and it is the one that explains why networks are deep.

280
00:26:15.400 --> 00:26:19.825
A single convolutional layer sees only the window of its kernel.

281
00:26:19.825 --> 00:26:21.950
Three residues, say.

282
00:26:21.950 --> 00:26:26.000
That is all one unit in layer one can possibly know about.

283
00:26:26.000 --> 00:26:28.725
But stack a second layer on top.

284
00:26:28.725 --> 00:26:34.700
A unit in layer two looks at several layer-one units, each of which looked at its own window of three.

285
00:26:34.700 --> 00:26:42.425
Those windows overlap but are offset, so the layer-two unit ultimately depends on five residues of the original input.

286
00:26:42.425 --> 00:26:45.175
Add a third layer and it is seven.

287
00:26:45.175 --> 00:26:56.300
The amount of original input one unit ultimately depends on is called its receptive field, which is the term we borrowed from Hubel and Wiesel's cats at the start of this block.

288
00:26:56.300 --> 00:27:03.450
The arithmetic is simple: with kernel size k and stride one, every layer adds k minus one positions.

289
00:27:03.450 --> 00:27:05.575
Three, five, seven.

290
00:27:05.575 --> 00:27:11.775
Pooling and larger strides make it grow much faster, because they shrink the data under each step.

291
00:27:11.775 --> 00:27:16.600
And this is how deep networks build complicated things out of simple ones.

292
00:27:16.600 --> 00:27:28.200
In images: layer one finds edges, layer two finds corners and simple shapes from combinations of edges, layer three finds textures and object parts, and so on up to whole objects.

293
00:27:28.200 --> 00:27:37.375
In DNA: layer one finds short motifs, layer two finds combinations of motifs, which is what regulatory grammar actually looks like.

294
00:27:37.375 --> 00:27:44.725
In protein: layer one finds a seven-residue pattern, layer two finds something the length of a structural element.

295
00:27:44.725 --> 00:27:53.525
Hold on to this, because in Block two we will compute the receptive field of our own network and find that it lands on a biologically meaningful length.

296
00:27:53.525 --> 00:28:01.350
The second discussion problem asks you to do exactly that: how wide must the window be if you want to see a whole signal peptide?

